        "jupyter: stats\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# Python Virtual Environments with `uv`\n",
        "\n",
        "A Python virtual environment is a project-specific Python installation plus a project-specific set of packages. It prevents one project from breaking another project when packages are installed or upgraded.\n",
        "\n",
        "For this course, the recommended tool is `uv`.\n",
        "\n",
        "[`uv`](https://docs.astral.sh/uv/) is a Python package and project manager written in Rust. It can:\n",
        "\n",
        "- install and manage Python versions,\n",
        "- create virtual environments,\n",
        "- install packages,\n",
        "- manage `pyproject.toml`,\n",
        "- create and use lock files,\n",
        "- run commands inside the project environment.\n",
        "\n",
        "You may still see `pip`, `venv`, and `conda` in Python tutorials. They are useful tools, but for this course `uv` is the default recommendation because it is fast, consistent, and works well with modern Python projects.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Package and Environment Tools\n",
        "\n",
        "There are several common tools in the Python ecosystem.\n",
        "\n",
        "| Tool | Main role |\n",
        "|---|---|\n",
        "| `uv` | Project manager, package installer, virtual environment manager, Python version manager. |\n",
        "| `pip` | Traditional Python package installer. |\n",
        "| `venv` | Built-in Python virtual environment tool. |\n",
        "| `conda` | Environment and package manager, especially common in scientific computing. |\n",
        "\n",
        "`pip` and `venv` are standard Python tools. `conda` is common in data science and can install non-Python libraries. `uv` covers the workflow we need in this course and is the tool emphasized here.\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.callout-note}\n",
        "## Recommendation\n",
        "\n",
        "Use `uv` for this course unless you have a strong reason to use `conda`.\n",
        "\n",
        "If you already use `conda` successfully, you do not need to remove it. Just avoid mixing tools inside the same environment unless you know why.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "## Installing `uv`\n",
        "\n",
        "Install `uv` from the official instructions:\n",
        "\n",
        "<https://docs.astral.sh/uv/getting-started/installation/>\n",
        "\n",
        "After installation, check that it works:\n",
        "\n",
        "```{.bash}\n",
        "uv --version\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "On Windows, you may need to restart VS Code or open a new terminal after installation.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Creating a Project Environment\n",
        "\n",
        "In a project folder, create a virtual environment with:\n",
        "\n",
        "```{.bash}\n",
        "uv venv\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "By default, this creates a folder named `.venv`.\n",
        "\n",
        "The `.venv` folder contains the Python environment for the project. It should usually not be committed to Git.\n",
        "\n",
        "To request a specific Python version:\n",
        "\n",
        "```{.bash}\n",
        "uv venv --python 3.12\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "If that Python version is not installed, `uv` can usually download and manage it.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Activating the Environment\n",
        "\n",
        "On Windows Command Prompt:\n",
        "\n",
        "```{.bash}\n",
        ".venv\\Scripts\\activate\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "On Windows PowerShell:\n",
        "\n",
        "```{.bash}\n",
        ".venv\\Scripts\\Activate.ps1\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "On macOS or Linux:\n",
        "\n",
        "```{.bash}\n",
        "source .venv/bin/activate\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "After activation, your terminal prompt usually shows the environment name.\n",
        "\n",
        "To deactivate:\n",
        "\n",
        "```{.bash}\n",
        "deactivate\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.callout-note}\n",
        "## Activation is optional with `uv run`\n",
        "\n",
        "With `uv`, you can often avoid manual activation by using `uv run`.\n",
        "\n",
        "For example:\n",
        "\n",
        "```{.bash}\n",
        "uv run python\n",
        "uv run quarto render\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "`uv run` runs the command inside the project environment.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "## Installing Packages\n",
        "\n",
        "For this course, the main packages are:\n",
        "\n",
        "- `numpy`,\n",
        "- `pandas`,\n",
        "- `matplotlib`,\n",
        "- `scikit-learn`,\n",
        "- `statsmodels`,\n",
        "- `jupyter`,\n",
        "- `ipykernel`.\n",
        "\n",
        "Install them with:\n",
        "\n",
        "```{.bash}\n",
        "uv pip install numpy pandas matplotlib scikit-learn statsmodels jupyter ipykernel\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "This command installs packages into the current virtual environment.\n",
        "\n",
        "If the environment is not activated, use:\n",
        "\n",
        "```{.bash}\n",
        "uv pip install --python .venv numpy pandas matplotlib scikit-learn statsmodels jupyter ipykernel\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "In many project workflows, it is better to add dependencies to `pyproject.toml`:\n",
        "\n",
        "```{.bash}\n",
        "uv add numpy pandas matplotlib scikit-learn statsmodels jupyter ipykernel\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "This records the dependencies as part of the project.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Project Files: `pyproject.toml` and `uv.lock`\n",
        "\n",
        "Modern Python projects often use `pyproject.toml` to record project metadata and dependencies.\n",
        "\n",
        "A simple dependency section may look like:\n",
        "\n",
        "```{.toml}\n",
        "[project]\n",
        "dependencies = [\n",
        "    \"numpy\",\n",
        "    \"pandas\",\n",
        "    \"matplotlib\",\n",
        "    \"scikit-learn\",\n",
        "    \"statsmodels\",\n",
        "    \"jupyter\",\n",
        "    \"ipykernel\",\n",
        "]\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "`uv.lock` records exact package versions. This helps rebuild the same environment later.\n",
        "\n",
        "The usual files are:\n",
        "\n",
        "- `pyproject.toml`: project dependencies and settings;\n",
        "- `uv.lock`: exact locked package versions;\n",
        "- `.venv/`: local virtual environment folder.\n",
        "\n",
        "Usually `pyproject.toml` and `uv.lock` are committed to Git. The `.venv/` folder is not.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Syncing an Existing Project\n",
        "\n",
        "If a project already has `pyproject.toml` and `uv.lock`, use:\n",
        "\n",
        "```{.bash}\n",
        "uv sync\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "This creates or updates `.venv` so that it matches the lock file.\n",
        "\n",
        "Then run Python commands with:\n",
        "\n",
        "```{.bash}\n",
        "uv run python\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "or activate the environment and use `python` directly.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Running Jupyter with the Environment\n",
        "\n",
        "VS Code can usually detect `.venv` automatically. In a notebook, click `Select Kernel` and choose the `.venv` environment.\n",
        "\n",
        "If the environment does not appear, install and register an IPython kernel:\n",
        "\n",
        "```{.bash}\n",
        "uv run python -m ipykernel install --user --name stats --display-name \"Python (stats)\"\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "Here `stats` is the kernel name. The display name is what you see in VS Code or Jupyter.\n",
        "\n",
        "This is useful for Quarto documents that contain:\n",
        "\n",
        "```{.yaml}\n",
        "jupyter: stats\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "After registering the kernel, check available kernels with:\n",
        "\n",
        "```{.bash}\n",
        "uv run jupyter kernelspec list\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "## Common `uv` Commands\n",
        "\n",
        "Create a virtual environment:\n",
        "\n",
        "```{.bash}\n",
        "uv venv\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "Create a virtual environment with a specific Python version:\n",
        "\n",
        "```{.bash}\n",
        "uv venv --python 3.12\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "Install packages into the environment:\n",
        "\n",
        "```{.bash}\n",
        "uv pip install numpy pandas matplotlib scikit-learn\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "Add dependencies to the project:\n",
        "\n",
        "```{.bash}\n",
        "uv add numpy pandas matplotlib scikit-learn\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "Remove a dependency:\n",
        "\n",
        "```{.bash}\n",
        "uv remove scikit-learn\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "Synchronize from `pyproject.toml` and `uv.lock`:\n",
        "\n",
        "```{.bash}\n",
        "uv sync\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "Run a command inside the environment:\n",
        "\n",
        "```{.bash}\n",
        "uv run python\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "Run Quarto inside the environment:\n",
        "\n",
        "```{.bash}\n",
        "uv run quarto render\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "Show installed packages:\n",
        "\n",
        "```{.bash}\n",
        "uv pip list\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "## `uv pip` versus `uv add`\n",
        "\n",
        "There are two common ways to install packages.\n",
        "\n",
        "Use `uv pip install` for quick one-time installation:\n",
        "\n",
        "```{.bash}\n",
        "uv pip install scikit-learn\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "Use `uv add` when the package should be recorded as a project dependency:\n",
        "\n",
        "```{.bash}\n",
        "uv add scikit-learn\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "For course projects, `uv add` is usually better because it updates `pyproject.toml` and the lock file.\n",
        "\n",
        "For quick experiments, `uv pip install` is fine.\n",
        "\n",
        "## How `pip` Fits In\n",
        "\n",
        "`pip` is the traditional Python package installer.\n",
        "\n",
        "The classic workflow is:\n",
        "\n",
        "```{.bash}\n",
        "python -m venv .venv\n",
        ".venv\\Scripts\\activate\n",
        "pip install numpy pandas matplotlib scikit-learn\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "This still works. `uv` provides a faster and more project-oriented version of this workflow.\n",
        "\n",
        "Many `pip` commands have a `uv pip` equivalent:\n",
        "\n",
        "```{.bash}\n",
        "pip install scikit-learn\n",
        "uv pip install scikit-learn\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "For this course, prefer the `uv` version.\n",
        "\n",
        "## How `conda` Fits In\n",
        "\n",
        "`conda` is an environment and package manager often used in data science. It is especially useful when a project needs non-Python system libraries or packages that are easier to install from `conda-forge`.\n",
        "\n",
        "A typical `conda` workflow is:\n",
        "\n",
        "```{.bash}\n",
        "conda create --name stats python=3.12\n",
        "conda activate stats\n",
        "conda install -c conda-forge numpy pandas matplotlib scikit-learn jupyter\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "This is a reasonable alternative if your machine already uses `conda`.\n",
        "\n",
        "However, avoid mixing `conda`, `pip`, and `uv` casually inside the same environment. Pick one main tool for a project.\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.callout-caution}\n",
        "## Do not mix tools unnecessarily\n",
        "\n",
        "If a project is managed by `uv`, install project packages with `uv`.\n",
        "\n",
        "If a project is managed by `conda`, install packages with `conda` when possible.\n",
        "\n",
        "Mixing tools can make environments harder to reproduce.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "## Reproducibility\n",
        "\n",
        "The old `pip` workflow often used:\n",
        "\n",
        "```{.bash}\n",
        "pip freeze > requirements.txt\n",
        "pip install -r requirements.txt\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "This can work, but it is not ideal for project management.\n",
        "\n",
        "With `uv`, the preferred reproducibility workflow is:\n",
        "\n",
        "```{.bash}\n",
        "uv add numpy pandas matplotlib scikit-learn statsmodels jupyter ipykernel\n",
        "uv lock\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "Then another user can recreate the environment with:\n",
        "\n",
        "```{.bash}\n",
        "uv sync\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "This uses `pyproject.toml` and `uv.lock` instead of a manually maintained `requirements.txt`.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Recommended Course Setup\n",
        "\n",
        "For a new course project:\n",
        "\n",
        "1. Open the project folder in VS Code.\n",
        "2. Create the environment:\n",
        "\n",
        "```{.bash}\n",
        "uv venv --python 3.12\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "3. Add the course packages:\n",
        "\n",
        "```{.bash}\n",
        "uv add numpy pandas matplotlib scikit-learn statsmodels jupyter ipykernel\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "4. Register the Jupyter kernel if needed:\n",
        "\n",
        "```{.bash}\n",
        "uv run python -m ipykernel install --user --name stats --display-name \"Python (stats)\"\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "5. In VS Code, select the `.venv` or `\"Python (stats)\"` kernel.\n",
        "\n",
        "After this setup, Python, Jupyter, Quarto, and `sklearn` should use the same project environment."
